---
// ── SEO COPY REWRITE APPLIED, 16 September 2026 ───────────────────
//
// This page has been rewritten against
// strata-and-common-area-cleaning-copy-rewrite.md (workbook row 27, primary
// `strata cleaning services`). Section numbers in the comments below are that
// brief's.
//
// Implemented: the approved title and meta, a new H1 carrying the mapped
// phrase with the old one demoted into the hero lead, the mapped phrase in
// the opening paragraph, the approved CTA in both places, a NEW comparison
// table written for a committee (brief §19), the Strata Schemes Management
// Act 2015 named in non-branded FAQ 3, the two em dash recasts in non-branded
// FAQs 2 and 4, the renamed FAQ headings and the full-footprint town list.
//
// ⚠️ EVERY MAPPED TERM ON THIS ROW READS 0/mo, and the brief says plainly that
// the number should not be trusted: the live SERP on 16 September 2026 showed
// a busy national market competing on exactly these terms, citing the Act by
// name and publishing cover levels. Treat the zeros as a data gap to re-pull,
// not as evidence the page has no search value. The consequence for this
// page is that the brand and conversion work IS the work, which is why
// sixteen of the brief's sections are marked No change.
//
// NO BLOCKING CONFLICT on this page. The approved CTA fits it better than the
// live label did.
//
// Not done here, because they are not copy: the URL mismatch (the workbook
// wants /commercial-cleaning/strata-cleaning, which keeps `strata-cleaning`
// contiguous where the live slug splits it — a redirect decision for the
// build team), the canonical domain, FAQ schema, the two missing CTA buttons
// (the walkthrough section and the bin room section both end without one),
// the header's competing "Get an instant quote", and hiding the testimonial
// block until real quotes exist.
//
// Strata and common area cleaning — one of the THIRTEEN "by type of premises"
// leaf pages under the /commercial-cleaning/ hub (navigation.ts, "By type of
// premises" group). Built in one pass with its twelve siblings.
//
// NO CONTENT BRIEF EXISTS for any of the thirteen. All copy below is DRAFTED,
// not supplied. See src/pages/commercial-cleaning/office-cleaning.astro's header for the launch
// blockers shared across the whole cluster ([insured], [police-checked],
// pricing basis, whether commercial work crosses the NSW border).
//
// WHAT MAKES THIS PAGE DIFFERENT from its twelve siblings, structurally:
//   - `ServiceBlocks variant="list"` instead of `icon-grid` for the scope.
//     Strata scope is naturally one row per common area, and each row can
//     carry an `href` to the service that actually does that area (carpet
//     extraction for the corridors, pressure cleaning for the driveway).
//     No other page in the cluster has that cross-sell shape.
//   - `StoryMosaic` on "who we actually report to". Unique to strata: the
//     person reading this page is usually NOT the person who pays the
//     invoice, and no other premises type has that split.
//   - `Callout tone="warning"` on bin rooms, not a `note`. It warns the
//     committee off a specific decision (leaving the bin room out of scope
//     to save money), which is what `warning` is for per Callout's own docs.
//   - A ComparisonTable with ITS OWN ROWS, not the shared ones. The original
//     build left the table off, correctly: the shared rows are written for a
//     homeowner choosing a cleaner ("the person in your home"), and a strata
//     committee is not choosing someone to enter their home. Brief §19 makes
//     the opposite case and wins it — a committee is formally comparing
//     contractors before a vote, which is the exact job a table does, and BFD
//     §9.4 says comparisons belong in a table rather than in running text —
//     so the table is now built from rows this page already argues in prose.
//     See `comparisonTable` below.
//
// BLOCKS LAUNCH, specific to this page:
//   - Does TLB do strata work at all today, and at what scale? The brand
//     foundation document names homes, Airbnb and real estate agencies as
//     the three audiences and never mentions strata. This page may be
//     ambition rather than a service. Confirm before it ships.
//   - Bin room and waste work. Presenting or returning bins is the single
//     most common strata add-on and it is a real physical job. Bracketed in
//     the scope list.
//   - Whether TLB carries the insurance level strata managers typically
//     require of contractors, which is often higher than a domestic
//     cleaner's. Bracketed in a non-branded FAQ.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/sections/SiteHeader.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/sections/Hero.astro';
import TrustBar from '../../components/sections/TrustBar.astro';
import Backdrop from '../../components/ui/Backdrop.astro';
import TextBlock from '../../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import ServiceBlocks from '../../components/sections/ServiceBlocks.astro';
import StoryMosaic from '../../components/sections/StoryMosaic.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/sections/Callout.astro';
import ImageBand from '../../components/sections/ImageBand.astro';
import ContentGrid from '../../components/sections/ContentGrid.astro';
import TagCloud from '../../components/sections/TagCloud.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '../../components/sections/ComparisonTable.astro';
import GoogleReviewSlider from '../../components/sections/GoogleReviewSlider.astro';
import { googleBusinessProfileUrl, googleReviews } from '../../data/googleReviews';
import Faq from '../../components/sections/Faq.astro';
import CallToAction from '../../components/sections/CallToAction.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/sections/SiteFooter.astro';
import logoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-horizontal.png';
// This page's own frames are in src/assets/commercial_cleaning/premises/;
// the prompts are in that folder's IMAGE-PROMPTS.md.
// §2 backdrop, behind the Cream scrim — the homepage's, same as the hub.
import footerLogoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-square.png';
import {
  primaryNav,
  headerNav,
  headerSecondaryCta,
  footerServiceLinks,
  footerContact,
  footerCopyright,
  quoteCta,
} from '../../data/navigation';
import { comparisonColumns } from '../../data/comparison';
import { footprintLocationGroups, footprintSubheading } from '../../data/locations';
import { trustBarCards } from '../../data/trust';
import { commercialBusiness, relatedPremises, walkthroughCtaLabel } from '../../data/premises';
import { howItWorks, managedForYou, theLineBody } from '../../data/brand-lines';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
import { getPage } from '../../lib/cms';
import * as section from '../../lib/cms-schemas';
import { sized } from '../../lib/cms-schemas';

// §0 Metadata. "Strata cleaning" is the head term people actually search;
// "common area cleaning" is the phrase committees and managers use in their
// own paperwork. Both are in the title because they are the same page.
//
// In `pageMeta`, on "Strata Cleaning Services - Common Areas | TLB Cleaning":
// Brief §2. The approved title, verbatim, at 53 characters. It carries
// `strata cleaning services` contiguously and keeps common areas in the
// modifier, where `common area cleaning` is captured without splitting the
// primary. The live title split the mapped phrase across "and Common Area".
// In `pageMeta`, on `description`:
// Brief §3. The approved meta, verbatim, at 138 characters. The live one was
// 190 — the longest in the engagement — and would have been cut off mid
// sentence, most likely losing the committee report, which is the strongest
// thing in it.
//
// ⚠️ CHANGE REQUEST for the workbook owner, brief §3: the approved meta
// splits the mapped primary the same way the live one did, so the meta
// component cannot score full marks even once implemented, and it loses the
// committee report promise, which is this page's single best differentiator.
// Both pasted as approved rather than edited, per Rule 3. A version keeping
// the report line would serve this page better.
// In `pageMeta`, on `canonical`:
// ⚠️ Placeholder domain, same as every sibling page. Confirm before launch.

// §7 The primary CTA. Brief §7: the approved label describes what this page
// actually delivers better than the live one did. The closing block already
// promises "a written scope, a price per visit and a suggested schedule for
// the periodic work, ready to take to the committee", and branded FAQ 2 is
// entirely about producing a document a committee can vote on. This is that
// promise on a button. One primary CTA, repeated, per BFD §9.5.
//
// ⚠️ This page carries only TWO CTA buttons where most carry four — the
// walkthrough section and the bin room section both end without one. A
// conversion gap rather than a copy error, and a template job. Brief §7.

// §1 Hero. `split-single-image`, image right, matching the rest of the
// commercial cluster.
//
// ⚠️ GENERATED, NOT PHOTOGRAPHED. Same standing note as every premises page
// — see office-cleaning.astro's §1 comment.
//
// In `hero`, on `headingLines`:
// Brief §4. The exact phrase `strata cleaning services` is worth 15 points
// scored present or absent, and the extension is the page's actual argument
// rather than decoration: a committee member is the person who approves the
// spend, and a scope they can check is what the whole page is selling.
// In `hero`, on `lead`:
// Brief §5 and §6, in that order. The demoted H1 opens the lead — it speaks
// to the owner's motive, property value and first impressions, and
// complements rather than repeats the new H1's committee angle — and the
// hero paragraph follows. Hero has no separate subheading slot, so the two
// run as one lead. "Common area" is not lost: it returns in the §2
// definition and throughout the body.

// §2 Definition. Answer-target block for the focus keyword. Paragraph two
// does the thing this page exists to do that no sibling does: separate common
// property from lots, because the reader's first real question is what they
// are even allowed to buy on the body corporate's money.

// §3 Scope. `ServiceBlocks variant="list"` — one row per common area, each
// with an `href`. This is the shape strata scope naturally takes, and it is
// the one place in the cluster where the scope itself can carry the
// cross-sell honestly: the corridors really do need carpet extraction and
// the driveway really does need pressure cleaning, and both are separate
// services with their own pages.
//
// ⚠️ EVERY ITEM IS DRAFTED against standard strata practice, not supplied by
// TLB. Confirm each one. The bin room row carries a real bracket.
//
// In `areaItems`, on "Bin room and waste area" (description):
// ⚠️ Real bracket. Bin presentation is a physical job with its own
// roster and it is the most common strata add-on.

// §4 Who we report to. `StoryMosaic` and UNIQUE TO THIS PAGE in the cluster:
// on every other premises page the reader is the decision maker, and here
// they usually are not. A committee member, a strata manager and an on-site
// building manager each want a different thing from the same contract, and a
// page that talks to only one of them loses the other two.
//
// Array order IS the layout (see SECTIONS.md) — text, image, text, image.

// §5 Callout. `warning`, not `note` — it warns a committee off a specific
// decision rather than correcting a belief, which is the line Callout's own
// docs draw.

// §6 The three ways a strata scope goes wrong. `ContentGrid` with `feature`
// cells — three short diagnoses, which is a different shape from the
// six-item scope list above it and from the prose blocks either side.

// §7 Related pages. The other twelve premises types, the two commercial
// services and the hub, from src/data/premises.ts, with this page filtered
// out of its own band. Airbnb is appended as an extra: a strata building full
// of short-stay lots is a real and growing case, and it is the one link a
// committee member reading this page might genuinely need next.

// §9 Branded FAQs, in the CUSTOMER'S voice — here that voice is a committee
// member or a manager, which is why "we" means the building.
//
// NOTE: no ComparisonTable on this page, deliberately. See the file header.
// These FAQs carry the two comparison rows that survive the audience change
// (employed not subcontracted, and local) as answers instead, where they can
// be worded for a contractor relationship rather than for someone letting a
// cleaner into their home.

// §10 Non-branded FAQs, the long-tail block.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED. The legal framing below is the part to
// check, and it is deliberately kept general:
//   - In NSW, an owners corporation's duty to maintain and keep common
//     property in a state of good and serviceable repair sits in the Strata
//     Schemes Management Act 2015. Correct in substance. NO SECTION NUMBER IS
//     QUOTED here on purpose: a wrong or superseded section reference is
//     worse than none, and the honest answer for a reader who needs the
//     detail is their strata manager or NSW Fair Trading.
//   - Cleaning is maintenance of common property in practice, but whether a
//     specific task is the owners corporation's or a lot owner's depends on
//     the by-laws of that scheme. Hedged accordingly.
//   - Nothing here should be read as legal advice, and the last answer says
//     so plainly rather than in a disclaimer nobody reads.
//
// In `nonBrandedFaqItems`, on "How much does strata cleaning cost?" (answer):
// ⚠️ BRACKETED ON PURPOSE, same as every sibling page.
// In `nonBrandedFaqItems`, on "How often should common areas be cleaned?" (answer):
// Brief §25. BFD §9.2 bans em dashes outright. Recast with "such as",
// keeping every item.
// In `nonBrandedFaqItems`, on "Is the owners corporation required to clean common property?" (answer):
// Brief §25. The page already made the legal claim and already carried
// the correct disclaimer; naming the Act is the same move the
// commercial pressure cleaning page makes with the Protection of the
// Environment Operations Act 1997, so it is a house pattern this page
// was missing rather than a new risk. National competitors cite it by
// name, and a named statute is the single most citable thing a page can
// carry into an AI answer under BFD §14. Nine words, no new claim, and
// the "not legal advice" line stays exactly where it is. Still NO
// SECTION NUMBER, for the reason in the block comment above.
// In `nonBrandedFaqItems`, on "What is the difference between common property and a lot?" (answer):
// Brief §25, same rule, recast with commas, not a word lost.
// In `nonBrandedFaqItems`, on "Do strata cleaners need insurance and inductions?" (answer):
// ⚠️ Bracket. Strata managers commonly require a higher public
// liability level than a domestic cleaner carries, plus a contractor
// induction. Confirm what TLB holds.

// §10b Comparison table — NEW, brief §19, and the one section this rewrite
// adds rather than protects.
//
// This page had no table. Brief §19 makes the case for one: a committee is
// formally comparing contractors before a vote, which is the exact job a
// comparison table does, and BFD §9.4 says comparisons belong in a table
// rather than in running text. The page kept all its comparison in prose,
// which is why it stayed polite and never quite closed.
//
// THE ROWS ARE THIS PAGE'S OWN, not the shared ones from data/comparison.ts.
// The shared eight are written for a household choosing a domestic cleaner;
// rows about holiday lets, reminders and booking by text mean nothing to a
// committee. Every row below is drawn from something this page already says
// in prose, so the table introduces no new claim: the scope row from §16, the
// subcontracting row from branded FAQ 3, the invoicing row from branded FAQ
// 1, the reporting row from the residents-and-reporting block, and the
// periodic-work row from §16's third failure mode.
//
// The COLUMNS are the shared, approved ones from data/comparison.ts rather
// than the brief's own wording, so this table cannot drift from the site's
// other four. The brief's column names ("a national contractor", "a local
// sole operator") describe the same two models and its cell values read
// correctly under either heading.
//
// Neutral and factual per BFD §9.4: "whoever is rostered, often a
// subcontractor" describes a delivery model, it does not accuse anyone.
//
// ⚠️ NO VOLUME CLAIM ATTACHED. Unlike the new sections in earlier batches,
// this one is not justified by a keyword. It is justified by the journey
// stage and by the format rule that makes a missing comparison table on a
// Consider stage page a mandatory flag. Said plainly so nobody looks for the
// number later.

// §11 Closing CTA.

// CONTENT lives in src/content/pages/commercial-cleaning/strata-cleaning.yaml
// and is edited in Pages CMS ("Strata cleaning"). This file owns the layout. The
// notes above explain why each section says what it says; they stay here
// because Pages CMS rewrites the YAML on save and would drop them.
const page = await getPage(
  'commercial-cleaning/strata-cleaning',
  z.object({
    seo: section.seo,
    // The label of the page's main button. It links to the quote page in Site
    // settings. Empty falls back to the site-wide walkthrough label.
    ctaLabel: section.optional(section.text),
    hero: section.hero,
    // Its background photo sits behind the trust bar and this block together
    // (the Backdrop below), so it cannot be left empty.
    definition: section.textBlock.extend({ backgroundImage: section.backgroundPhoto }),
    // ServiceBlocks' list variant draws each row's icon, which the shared
    // linkList schema leaves out, so this page adds it.
    areas: section.linkList.extend({
      items: z.array(section.linkList.shape.items.element.extend({ icon: section.optional(section.serviceIcon) })).min(1),
    }),
    binRoom: section.callout,
    binRoomPhoto: section.imageBand,
    reporting: section.storyMosaic,
    scopeFailures: section.contentGrid,
    // The columns are the shared ones in Site settings; the rows are this page's.
    comparison: section.comparisonTable.omit({ columns: true }),
    // The pills are the other premises pages, from Site settings, plus this
    // page's own extra links (`tags`), which go in after them (§7 above).
    related: section.tagCloud.extend({ note: section.optional(z.array(section.text)) }),
    reviews: section.sectionHeading,
    // The subheading falls back to the shared footprint line, and the towns
    // come from Site settings.
    whereWeClean: section.tagCloud.omit({ tags: true }).extend({ subheading: section.optional(section.text) }),
    // Heading only. The paragraphs are the shared brand lines.
    brandLine: section.sectionHeading,
    brandedFaq: section.faq,
    faq: section.faq,
    closingCta: section.callToAction,
  }),
);

const pageMeta = {
  ...page.seo,
  canonical: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/commercial-cleaning/strata-cleaning/',
};

const walkthroughCta = { label: page.ctaLabel ?? walkthroughCtaLabel, href: quoteCta.href };

const heroImage = page.hero.image && sized(page.hero.image, '16/9');

const reportingBlocks = page.reporting.blocks.map((block) =>
  block.type === 'image' ? { type: 'image' as const, ...sized(block.image, '4/3') } : block,
);

const scopeFailureBlocks = page.scopeFailures.items.map((item) =>
  item.type === 'image' ? { type: 'image' as const, ...sized(item.image, '4/3') } : item,
);

const relatedTags = relatedPremises('/commercial-cleaning/strata-cleaning/', page.related.tags ?? []);

const business = commercialBusiness(pageMeta.description);
---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="split-single-image"
      imagePosition="right"
      kicker={page.hero.kicker}
      headingLines={page.hero.headingLines}
      subheading={page.hero.subheading}
      lead={page.hero.lead}
      cta={page.hero.cta ?? walkthroughCta}
      image={heroImage}
    />

    <Backdrop image={page.definition.backgroundImage} tint="muted" opacity={90}>
      <TrustBar variant="card" cards={trustBarCards} />
      <TextBlock
        theme="muted"
        align="left"
        heading={page.definition.heading}
        body={page.definition.body}
        cta={page.definition.cta}
      />
    </Backdrop>

    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="list"
      heading={page.areas.heading}
      lead={page.areas.lead}
      note={page.areas.note}
      items={page.areas.items}
    />

    <Callout tone="warning" heading={page.binRoom.heading} body={page.binRoom.body} cta={page.binRoom.cta} />

    {/* The photo the callout above is arguing for. `ImageBand`'s caption is
        the whole point of the component, and this is the one place on the
        cluster where a single image carries an argument by itself: a bin
        room that looks like this is the outcome being sold.

        ⚠️ Source is 4:3 and the box is 16/9, so `cover` crops the top and
        bottom a little. Checked against the frame: the bins, the drain and
        the wet floor all survive the crop, which is everything the caption
        claims. Regenerate at 16:9 if this is ever reshot. */}
    <ImageBand
      ratio="16/9"
      label={page.binRoomPhoto.image.label}
      caption={page.binRoomPhoto.caption}
      src={page.binRoomPhoto.image.src}
    />

    <StoryMosaic theme="dark" heading={page.reporting.heading} blocks={reportingBlocks} cta={page.reporting.cta} />

    <ContentGrid heading={page.scopeFailures.heading} lead={page.scopeFailures.lead} columns={3} blocks={scopeFailureBlocks} />

    {/* Brief §19, inserted immediately after the scope-failure grid exactly
        as the brief specifies: the reader has just been given three ways to
        audit their current contract, and this is where they turn that on the
        quotes in front of them. Rows and reasoning at `comparisonTable`. */}
    <ComparisonTable
      heading={page.comparison.heading}
      cornerLabel={page.comparison.cornerLabel}
      lead={page.comparison.lead}
      columns={comparisonColumns}
      rows={page.comparison.rows}
      footnote={page.comparison.footnote}
      cta={page.comparison.cta}
    />

    {/* ⚠️ RESERVED SLOT: before and after slider (BeforeAfter.astro).

        THE PHOTO BRIEF for this page: a bin room, and a driveway or entry
        path with algae on it. Both from a locked-off tripod, before and
        after, nothing moved between frames. Those two are the highest-value
        pair on the whole cluster, because they are the two jobs a committee
        is most likely to have been told are not worth paying for. Drop it in
        here, between the scope-failure grid and the related pages. */}

    <TagCloud
      heading={page.related.heading}
      subheading={page.related.subheading}
      tags={relatedTags}
      note={page.related.note ?? []}
    />

    <GoogleReviewSlider heading={page.reviews.heading} reviews={googleReviews} profileUrl={googleBusinessProfileUrl} />

    {/* Brief §20, the full footprint rather than the six-town NSW list. The
        hero promises the Tweed and this band dropped it, against BFD §2.1,
        and there is a category reason too: strata density in this footprint
        is concentrated in Tweed Heads, Kingscliff, Coolangatta and Burleigh,
        and the page named none of them while selling apartment block
        cleaning. The navigation on this very page already carries this exact
        list, so it is a template fix rather than a writing task. */}
    <TagCloud
      heading={page.whereWeClean.heading}
      subheading={page.whereWeClean.subheading ?? footprintSubheading}
      groups={footprintLocationGroups}
      note={page.whereWeClean.note}
    />

    {/* §1.3 The Line as a section heading, with §1.4 as its standfirst.
        The copy, and the reason this page gets `fast` rather than the other
        variant, both live in data/brand-lines.ts. Change it there, not here.

        `muted`, not `dark`: TagCloud above paints --color-brand and Faq below
        paints --color-surface, so Cream between them keeps the page
        alternating instead of stacking two dark bands.

        No CTA prop. §9.5 wants one primary CTA per page, repeated, and the
        closing CallToAction is directly under the FAQs. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="muted"
      align="left"
      heading={page.brandLine.heading}
      body={[managedForYou.recurring, ...theLineBody.commercial, ...howItWorks.general]}
    />

    {/* Brief §22 and §23. The page carries two FAQ headings and the generic
        one made the split look like a build error: this block is about
        working with TLB, the next is about the category. The second heading
        also lands the mapped phrase contiguously, which is the 10-point H2
        component the page previously half scored, and drops Title Case,
        which BFD §9.2 bans. */}
    <Faq heading={page.brandedFaq.heading} lead={page.brandedFaq.lead} items={page.brandedFaq.items} />
    <Faq heading={page.faq.heading} lead={page.faq.lead} items={page.faq.items} />

    <CallToAction
      variant="secondary"
      kicker={page.closingCta.kicker}
      heading={page.closingCta.heading}
      lead={page.closingCta.lead}
      cta={page.closingCta.cta ?? walkthroughCta}
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
